/* Chapter 4 — From Precontemplation to Contemplation: Building Readiness. Source: SAMHSA TIP 35 (2019), pp. 65–81. Study content only from the book. */
window.MI_CHAPTERS.push({
  id: 4,
  title: 'From Precontemplation to Contemplation: Building Readiness',
  pages: '65–81',
  quote: { text: 'The task for individuals in Precontemplation is to become conscious of and concerned about the current pattern of behavior and/or interested in a new behavior. From a change perspective, it is more important to recognize an individual’s current views on change and address her or his reasons for not wanting to change than it is to understand how the status quo came to be.', by: 'DiClemente, 2018, p. 29' },
  keyMessages: [
    'In the Precontemplation stage, clients do not recognize that they have a problem with substance use, or they recognize the problem but are not ready to change their substance use behaviors.',
    'Counselors should be nonjudgmental about clients’ low motivation to change and instead focus on building a strong working alliance.',
    'A key strategy to help clients move from Precontemplation to contemplating change is to raise their level of concern and awareness of the risk associated with their current substance use behaviors.',
    'Involving family members and significant others (SOs) can increase clients’ concern about substance use.'
  ],
  sections: [
    { id: 'intro', h: 'Precontemplation and How Clients Arrive', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Clients who are unconcerned about their substance use, or concerned but not considering change, are in <strong>Precontemplation</strong>. They may stay there or in early Contemplation for years. A client in Precontemplation is often moved into the cycle of change by <strong>extrinsic</strong> sources of motivation, for example:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'A college coach refers an athlete who tests positive for cocaine.',
        'A wife insists she’ll file for divorce unless her husband gets treatment for his drinking.',
        'A tenant is displaced from federal housing because of substance use.',
        'The court refers a driver for driving while intoxicated.',
        'A woman tests positive for substances at a prenatal visit.',
        'An employer sends an employee with declining performance to the employee assistance program, which refers him for treatment.',
        'An emergency physician finds alcohol in the system of a driver in a serious crash.',
        'A family physician suggests treatment after a high AUDIT score.',
        'A mother learns she cannot regain custody of her children from Child Protective Services until she stops using and seeks treatment.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'In each case, someone important to the person voiced concern. The person is better motivated to abstain or moderate when concerned others offer relevant information in a <strong>supportive and empathic</strong> way rather than a judgmental, dismissive or confrontational one.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 4.1 — Counseling strategies for Precontemplation' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Area', 'Content'], rows: [
        ['Client motivation', 'Not concerned about substance use or unaware of problems; not yet considering change, or unwilling or unable to change; often pressured by others to seek help.'],
        ['Counselor focus', 'Develop rapport and build trust for a strong alliance. Raise doubts and concerns about the client’s substance use. Understand special motivational considerations for clients mandated to treatment.'],
        ['Counseling strategies', 'Elicit the client’s perceptions of the problem. Explore the events that led to treatment. Assess SOC stage and readiness. Commend the client for coming. Agree on a direction. Provide information about effects and risks. Evoke concern. Give personalized feedback on assessment findings. Involve SOs. Express concern and leave the door open.']
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'rapport', h: 'Develop Rapport and Build Trust', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Before raising change with people who are not thinking about it, establish rapport and trust in a safe, supportive setting. <strong>As clients become more engaged, their defensiveness and reluctance to change decrease.</strong> Strategies for initial conversations:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        '<strong>Ask permission</strong> to address changing substance use — this respects autonomy.',
        'Explain how you and the program work: session length, what you hope to accomplish now and over time, any assessments needed. <strong>Don’t overwhelm</strong> a new client with all the rules.',
        'Raise <strong>confidentiality</strong> up front: what stays private, what can be released with permission, what must go back to a referring agency.',
        'Explain that you will not tell the client what to do or whether to change; the client will do most of the talking. Invite comments on what the client hopes to achieve.',
        'Ask why the client has come, mention what you know, and ask for the client’s version. If the client is hesitant or defensive, pick a topic of interest (e.g., a stressor such as illness, marital discord, heavy workload) that links to substance use: “How does your use of alcohol fit into this?”',
        'Avoid calling it the client’s <strong>“problem”</strong> or <strong>“substance misuse”</strong> — this may not reflect the client’s view, and labels raise defenses.',
        'Align your approach to the client’s SOC stage; if the client is already contemplating or committed, move to later-stage strategies.'
      ] },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Counselor note: agency policy about client intoxication', x: 'In the first session, be transparent about your agency’s policy on sessions with intoxicated clients and what you will do. Intoxication impairs the ability to participate in any treatment. Many programs use breathalyzer or urine tests and reschedule if substances are detected or the client appears under the influence. If a client is intoxicated, ask him or her <strong>nonjudgmentally to leave, reschedule, and help the client get home safely</strong>.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'perception', h: 'Elicit the Client’s Perception and Explore What Led to Treatment', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Invite clients to explain their understanding of the problem — direct but nonjudgmental: “Can you tell me a bit about what brings you here today?” or “I’d like to understand your perspective on why you’re here. Can we start there?”' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Start exploring the events that led to treatment by <strong>recognizing the client’s emotional state</strong>. After an arrest, a confrontation or a health crisis, clients may feel shaken, angry, withdrawn, ashamed, terrified or relieved — often a mix. <strong>Strong emotions become obstacles to change if you don’t acknowledge them through reflective listening.</strong>' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Focus on the client’s recent experience and what matters to him or her: the athlete’s participation and performance, the employee’s job, the driver’s license or freedom, the pregnant woman’s healthy child, the mother’s custody, the husband’s need for guidance on getting his spouse into treatment.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Many seek treatment because of <strong>external pressure</strong>, and some blame the referral source. Start with those external sources to raise awareness of the impact on others: “What kind of things seem to bother her?” “What do you think makes her believe there is a problem associated with your drinking?” If perceptions differ, invite the wife to a session. If the client thinks the probation officer is the problem: <strong>“Why do you think your probation officer believes you have a problem?”</strong> Reflect, and avoid agreeing or disagreeing with the client’s position.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'assess', h: 'Assess the Client’s SOC and Readiness to Change', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Determine readiness and SOC stage at the first meeting — it decides which strategies will work. <strong>Don’t assume</strong> that a client with obvious signs of an SUD is already contemplating or ready: a new client can be anywhere on the severity continuum, have few or many problems, and be at any stage.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'The Importance and Confidence Rulers' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'The simplest readiness assessment. The <strong>Importance Ruler</strong> shows how important change is right now; the <strong>Confidence Ruler</strong> shows self-efficacy for change right now. Together they indicate readiness. Clients in Precontemplation typically score <strong>0 to 3</strong>. Scores are neither fixed nor always linear — clients move forward, backward or jump; your role is to facilitate movement toward positive change.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 4.2 — Styles of Precontemplation: the 5 Rs (DiClemente, 2018)' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Clients not yet contemplating change express sustain talk in one or more of five ways. Identify the style and tailor your approach.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Style', 'Client presentation', 'Counseling approach'], rows: [
        ['Reveling', 'Still focused on good experiences with use; few negative consequences yet.', 'Objective, nonjudgmental feedback about use, health risks and other consequences to raise doubt that they can avoid negative effects.'],
        ['Reluctance', 'Lack knowledge of the dimensions of the problem or its personal impact to think change is necessary.', 'Nonjudgmental feedback on how use affects their lives; reassurance that they can function without the addictive behavior.'],
        ['Rebellion', 'Afraid of losing control; large investment in their substance of choice.', 'Help them make positive choices rather than rebel against pressure; <strong>emphasize personal choice and responsibility</strong>.'],
        ['Resignation', 'Hopeless, helpless, overwhelmed by the energy change takes; many prior treatments or failed attempts.', 'Restore hope and optimism: explore specific barriers and successful changes in other behaviors; share how treatment has helped people who thought they couldn’t change; link to others in recovery.'],
        ['Rationalization', 'Think they have all the answers; use is a problem for others, not them.', '<strong>Double-sided reflection</strong> rather than arguing; acknowledge what they say but point out reservations they voiced earlier.']
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'instruments', h: 'Readiness Assessment Instruments', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Instruments give scores matching levels of readiness; discussing specific answers can also raise awareness of use and of what is getting in the way. Full versions are in Appendix B.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Instrument', 'Key facts'], rows: [
        ['URICA (University of Rhode Island Change Assessment Scale)', 'Developed for psychotherapy; adapted for addiction; <strong>the most common clinical measure of SOC stage</strong>. <strong>32 items</strong>, 8 per stage subscale (Precontemplation, Contemplation, Action, Maintenance), rated 1 (strong disagreement) to 5 (strong agreement). Client names a specific “problem” (e.g., cocaine use) and answers with it in mind; may complete it more than once for several problems. Repeat periodically to track movement.'],
        ['SOCRATES (Stages of Change Readiness and Treatment Eagerness Scale)', 'Measures readiness. Original 32 items; <strong>19-item clinical version</strong>, self-administered. <strong>8A for alcohol, 8D for drugs.</strong> Assesses problem recognition, ambivalence and taking steps. Scores are fed back as a starting point for discussion; changes over time show an intervention’s impact.'],
        ['Readiness To Change Questionnaire', 'For healthcare providers who are not addiction specialists, assessing clients misusing alcohol. <strong>12 items</strong> adapted from the URICA, mapped to <strong>3 stages</strong> — Precontemplation (“It’s a waste of time thinking about my drinking because I do not have a problem”), Contemplation (“Sometimes I think I should quit or cut down on my drinking”), Action (“I am actively working on my drinking problem”). 5-point scale.']
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'commend', h: 'Commend the Client and Agree on a Direction', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Affirming responsible behaviors like entering treatment increases confidence that change is possible. Referred clients may feel little control; some expect blame, some expect a cure, some hope counseling solves everything with little effort. Whatever they expect, <strong>affirm their courage</strong>: “It took you a lot of effort to get here. You are determined to figure out what’s going on and how you can change things.” To a mother who came rather than risk losing custody: “You must care very much about your child.”' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'With clients not yet thinking seriously of change, plan strategies carefully and find an acceptable pathway together. Advice may be appropriate, but <strong>always ask permission</strong> first: “I’d like to tell you about what we could do here. Would that be all right?” Express different viewpoints supportively, not authoritatively. <strong>You don’t need to agree on treatment goals at the beginning</strong> — use strategies like agenda mapping to agree on how to proceed in the current conversation.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Throughout, use OARS and person-centered principles. Emphasizing personal autonomy shows you are not pressuring the client to change.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'information', h: 'Raise Doubts: Provide Information About Effects and Risks', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Once rapport is established, raise doubts and concerns. Psychoeducation can increase ambivalence and move clients toward contemplation.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Give basic information early if clients haven’t had drug and alcohol education.',
        'Use <strong>EPE</strong> for a joint discussion rather than a lecture.',
        'Ask permission (“Would it be okay to tell you a bit about the effects of ___?”) or ask what they already know.',
        'Talk about what happens to <strong>any user</strong> of the substance, not just the client.',
        'State what <strong>experts have found</strong>, not what you think happens.',
        'Give small chunks, then elicit understanding: “What do you make of all this?”',
        'Describe addiction in <strong>biological terms</strong> — facts can increase hope and readiness: “…your mind begins to believe that you need these substances in the same way you need… food… You’re not stronger than this process, but you can be smarter, and you can regain your independence from substances.”'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Other useful facts: drivers may be surprised how few drinks meet legal intoxication; women hoping to conceive may not know substances can diminish fertility and harm the fetus before they know they are pregnant; clients may not realize alcohol interacts with medications for depression or hypertension.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Expert comment (Jeffrey M. Georgi): liver transplantation', x: 'A client with end-stage liver disease from alcoholic hepatitis and cirrhosis, being evaluated for transplant, may not see himself as having an alcohol problem despite overwhelming evidence — from guilt, fear of not being listed, or simply never having connected his health with drinking. <strong>Confronting him with the evidence only increases defensiveness, denial, guilt and shame.</strong> Instead: connect with his history without excessive self-disclosure, ask about the positive side of his use, and after a thorough assessment give a medical perspective on alcohol dependence (brain chemistry, reward, tolerance, genetics), tailored to education level — comparing it to diabetes if needed. Seeing himself in that picture often leads to <strong>self-diagnosis</strong>, and self-diagnosis is itself the move from Precontemplation to Contemplation. The psychoeducational reframe removes the guilt of seeing the disease as a moral failing.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Counselor note: motivational language in written materials', x: 'Brochures, flyers and educational materials can help clients think differently about change. Judgmental words like <strong>“abuse” or “denial”</strong> are as off-putting in writing as in session. Use plain language with motivation in mind; a brochure that opens with a long list of rules may scare clients away. Review materials from the client’s viewpoint.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'concern', h: 'Evoke Concern About the Client’s Substance Use', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Raise doubts about the harmlessness of use. As clients acknowledge some problems, they become more aware of conflict and feel <strong>greater ambivalence</strong> — and change becomes increasingly possible.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Explore the <strong>“positive” and “less-positive”</strong> aspects of use:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Start with the client’s view of the “benefits,” then move to less-beneficial aspects — rather than just asking about problems.',
        'Don’t focus only on negatives, or the client ends up defending use while you push for unwanted change.',
        'Don’t spend too long on the “good” things — that reinforces sustain talk, which is linked to lower motivation and worse outcomes.',
        'Show you understand why the client “values” use: “Help me understand what you like about your drinking. What do you enjoy about it?” then <strong>“What do you like less about drinking?”</strong> A client who can name nothing he “likes less” is probably not ready and may need more information.',
        'Summarize in personal language so the client hears the developing ambivalence.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Respond with <strong>double-sided reflections</strong>: “So, drinking helps you relax. Yet, you say you sometimes resent all the money you are spending, and it’s hard for you to get to work on time, especially Monday mornings.”' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Ask how use affects different areas of life (“How is your drinking affecting your family life?”) and use <strong>balanced reflective listening</strong>: “Help me understand. You’ve been saying you see no need to change, and you are concerned about losing your family. I don’t see how this fits together. I’m wondering if this is confusing for you, too.”' }
    ] },
    { id: 'feedback', h: 'Provide Personalized Feedback on Assessment Findings', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Personalized feedback is effective for raising doubt. In brief interventions it is short and based on screening; in specialty treatment it can cover a comprehensive assessment:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Substance use patterns and history.',
        'DSM-5 diagnostic criteria for SUDs.',
        'General functioning and its links to substance use.',
        'Health and biomedical effects (e.g., sleep disorders, HIV, diabetes).',
        'Neuropsychological effects of long-term use.',
        'Family history of mental disorders and SUDs (risk for SUDs and CODs).',
        'CODs and the effects of substance use on mental illness.',
        'Functional analysis of substance use triggers.'
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Give feedback on the client’s own risks and how consumption compares with others of the <strong>same culture, age or gender</strong>. Many clients then see the gap between where they are and where their values lie.' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Make sure the client values the information and believes it will help.',
        'If possible, schedule formal assessments <strong>after at least one session</strong>, or have a brief MI conversation before and after the assessment.',
        'Explain what tests will be given, what they show and how long they take; adapt the format (first-language self-report forms; face-to-face interview if there are cognitive challenges).',
        'Review findings in a way that raises awareness and develops discrepancy with values (the MET manual in Appendix C has a sample personal feedback report).',
        'Divide extensive feedback into small chunks with <strong>EPE</strong>. <strong>One or two pieces of feedback may be enough</strong> to move a client toward Contemplation.'
      ] },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Counselor note: description of a typical day', x: 'An informal way to engage, build rapport and learn the context of use (e.g., how much of the day goes to getting drugs and how little to loved ones). Start by <strong>asking permission</strong>: “Would it be okay if we spend a few minutes going through a typical day from beginning to end?” Be curious. <strong>Avoid the word “problem”</strong> unless the client uses it. Follow the whole day, asking about behaviors and feelings — “What happens when…?” Ask slowly, don’t add your own explanations, let the client use his or her own words, and ask for clarification only for unfamiliar terms or missing information.' }
    ] },
    { id: 'sos', h: 'Involve Significant Others', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Many people with SUDs respond to motivation from spouses and SOs to enter treatment. An SO is typically a parent, spouse, live-in partner or other family member, but can be anyone with a close personal relationship to the client. Supportive SOs help clients become <strong>intrinsically</strong>, not just extrinsically, motivated. Involving them is <strong>cost effective</strong> and linked to more change talk, stronger commitment, and less substance use and fewer alcohol-related consequences.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'SOs can increase readiness by reminding clients of the importance of family and the relationship; giving helpful feedback on negative effects; encouraging change; pointing to coping resources that support recovery; and positively reinforcing use of those resources.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Expert comment (Allen Zweben): involving an SO', x: 'First determine that the SO has a <strong>positive relationship</strong> with the client and a genuine investment in the change process, and assess whether the SO supports the client’s motivational statements. Then ask questions that help the SO feel optimistic: “Have you noticed what efforts Jack has made to change his drinking?” “What has been most helpful to you in helping Jack deal with the drinking?” “What is different now that leads you to feel better about Jack’s ability to change?”' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Before involving an SO' },
      { t: 'ul', items: ['Ask the client’s permission to contact the SO.', 'Describe the benefits of SO support.', 'Review confidentiality concerns.', 'If the client agrees, obtain the written releases.'] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Engaging an SO in the first joint meeting' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Use MI strategies with the SO; praise the SO’s willingness to take part.',
        'Offer conversation guidelines (use “I” statements; no blaming or shaming language).',
        'Define the SO’s role (emotional/instrumental support, helpful feedback, reinforcing reasons for change).',
        'Be optimistic about the SO’s support; invite the SO onto the client’s team.',
        'Teach the SO briefly to ask open questions, reflect and support change talk.',
        'Invite the SO to identify family values and how use might not fit them.',
        'Reinforce positive SO comments; <strong>refocus</strong> if the SO’s feedback is negative or reinforces sustain talk.',
        'Use EPE to tell the SO about support services for his or her own recovery (e.g., Al-Anon, family peer support, individual counseling).',
        'If the SO can’t be supportive and nonconfrontational or has his or her own substance or behavioral health problems, <strong>limit the role to information sharing</strong> and refer the SO for services.',
        'If the SO can’t attend, invite the SO <strong>figuratively</strong>: “You have mentioned that your relationship with your daughter is very important to you. How would not drinking impact the quality of your relationship?”'
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'door', h: 'Express Concern, and Leave the Door Open', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'If the client remains in Precontemplation and you cannot agree on treatment goals:' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Summarize your concern based on screening, assessment or SO feedback.',
        'Present feedback factually and nonjudgmentally.',
        'Remind the client you respect his or her decision, even if the data suggest a different choice; emphasize personal choice to keep rapport.',
        'Make sure the client has your contact details and crisis or emergency contacts before ending.',
        'Ask permission for a <strong>brief check-in call in a month</strong>; if the client agrees, follow up — a chance to reassess and encourage return.'
      ] }
    ] },
    { id: 'mandated', h: 'Clients Mandated to Treatment', blocks: [
      { t: 'p', x: 'Clients may be ordered to attend by an employer, an employee assistance program or the criminal justice system, with consequences for not entering or completing treatment (job loss, revoked probation or parole, prosecution, imprisonment). Many are in Precontemplation, but the temptation is to use <strong>Action stage interventions</strong> immediately — this is <strong>counterproductive</strong>. They arrive with strong emotions about the referral.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Mandated clients tend to produce a great deal of <strong>sustain talk</strong>, which predicts poorer outcomes. A key strategy is to <strong>lessen or “soften” sustain talk before trying to evoke change talk</strong>. Even so, mandated clients have <strong>similar outcomes to voluntary clients</strong> when stage-appropriate motivational strategies are used.' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'You may spend the first session <strong>“decontaminating” the referral</strong> — some counselors say, “I’m sorry you came through the door this way.” Principles: honor the client’s anger and sense of powerlessness; avoid assumptions about the treatment needed; make clear you will help the client explore what he or she sees as useful.' },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Information sharing with the referring agency' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Formalize release of information with a <strong>written consent</strong> that meets federal confidentiality regulations.',
        'Tell clients what will be released (e.g., attendance, urine test results, participation) and get consent.',
        'Make sure clients know which choices they have and which are not yours or theirs to make (e.g., child abuse or neglect).',
        'Consider the role of the client’s attorney, if any; delineate levels of permission.',
        'Be clear about the consequences of not participating.'
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Presenting consequences while keeping the alliance (Stinson & Clark, 2017)' },
      { t: 'ul', items: [
        'Acknowledge ambivalence about participating.',
        'Differentiate your role from the referring agency’s authority: “I am here to help you make some decisions about how you might want to change, not to pressure you to change.”',
        'Describe consequences in a neutral, nonjudgmental tone.',
        '<strong>Don’t side</strong> with the client or the agency about fairness; stay neutral.',
        'Emphasize personal choice: “It’s up to you whether you participate in treatment.”'
      ] },
      { t: 'h3', x: 'Exhibit 4.3 — Opening dialog with a client mandated to treatment' },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Jeff, a counselor in a CBT-based outpatient program, meets Paul, required to attend group as a condition of parole with a 4-year sentence hanging over him.' },
      { t: 'table', head: ['Moment', 'What the counselor does'], rows: [
        ['Opening', '“I got some information from your probation officer, but what would really help me is to hear from you…” (open question as a statement)'],
        ['Paul: “Get that probation officer off my back.”', 'Expresses confusion and asks an open question: “What is it that I can do that might help?”'],
        ['Paul: “Tell her I don’t need to be here.”', 'States honestly that this is not an option for either of them; affirms Paul’s commitment to himself and his family; notes he doesn’t want prison.'],
        ['Reframe', '“It seems to me you’ve made some good choices so far” — he could have skipped the appointment but didn’t (affirmation, personal autonomy).'],
        ['“Do I still have to come?”', '“I don’t have the power to make that kind of decision. However, you and I can work together to figure out how you might use this course to benefit you.” (partnership)'],
        ['Paul dismisses the group', 'Affirms (“You just don’t seem like a whiner to me”), explains the group teaches skills, suggests taking it <strong>one group at a time</strong> with an open mind, and supports his three goals: family, self, freedom.'],
        ['Outcome', 'Paul agrees to try: “This wasn’t as bad as I thought it would be.”']
      ] },
      { t: 'p', x: 'Although the program is CBT-based, the counselor works <strong>in the spirit of MI</strong> — partnership, acceptance, affirmations, a neutral nonjudgmental tone, and emphasis on autonomy and values.' },
      { t: 'note', title: 'Conclusion', x: 'With clients in Precontemplation: first develop rapport and a counseling alliance; next assess readiness; then help them become aware that substance use is linked to problems in their lives. MET strategies (personalized feedback) and MI strategies (reflective listening, personal choice and responsibility, exploring discrepancy) suit the move to Contemplation.' }
    ] }
  ],
  highYield: [
    'Precontemplation: not recognizing a problem, or recognizing it but not ready to change. Clients often enter through <strong>extrinsic</strong> pressure.',
    'Counselor focus in Precontemplation: rapport and trust → raise doubts and concerns → special care for mandated clients.',
    'As engagement increases, defensiveness and reluctance to change decrease.',
    'Avoid the words “problem,” “substance misuse,” “abuse” and “denial” with clients in Precontemplation — labels raise defenses.',
    'Acknowledge the client’s emotional state through reflective listening; unacknowledged strong emotions block change.',
    '“Why do you think your probation officer believes you have a problem?” lets the client voice the referral source’s view.',
    'Don’t assume an obvious SUD means the client is ready — assess stage and readiness first.',
    'Precontemplators usually score <strong>0–3</strong> on the Importance and Confidence Rulers.',
    '5 Rs: Reveling (feedback), Reluctance (feedback + reassurance), Rebellion (personal choice), Resignation (restore hope), Rationalization (double-sided reflection).',
    'URICA: 32 items, 4 stages, most common clinical stage measure. SOCRATES: 19-item clinical version, 8A alcohol / 8D drugs. Readiness To Change Questionnaire: 12 items, 3 stages, for non-specialists.',
    'Affirm the courage it took to come; always ask permission before advice; no need to agree on treatment goals at the start.',
    'Information-giving: EPE, talk about any user, state what experts found, small chunks, explain addiction biologically.',
    'Explore “likes” then “likes less”; a client who can name nothing he likes less is probably not ready.',
    'One or two pieces of personalized feedback may be enough to move a client toward Contemplation.',
    'Typical day: ask permission, be curious, avoid “problem,” follow behaviors and feelings through the whole day.',
    'SOs help clients become intrinsically motivated; involve them only with permission, releases and a positive relationship.',
    'If the SO is negative or impaired, limit the role to information sharing; if absent, invite the SO figuratively.',
    'If the client stays in Precontemplation: express concern, respect the decision, give contacts, ask permission for a 1-month check-in call.',
    'Mandated clients: avoid Action stage interventions; soften sustain talk before evoking change talk; outcomes similar to voluntary clients.',
    '“Decontaminate” the referral; differentiate your role from the agency’s; stay neutral about fairness of consequences.'
  ],
  cards: [
    { id: 'c01', tag: 'Precontemplation', q: 'Define the Precontemplation stage as TIP 35 describes it.', a: 'Clients do not recognize a problem with substance use, or recognize it but are not ready to change.' },
    { id: 'c02', tag: 'Precontemplation', q: 'What usually moves a client in Precontemplation into the cycle of change?', a: 'Extrinsic sources of motivation — a spouse, employer, court, coach, physician or child protective services voicing concern.' },
    { id: 'c03', tag: 'Precontemplation', q: 'What makes concerned others’ feedback more likely to motivate change?', a: 'Offering relevant information in a supportive, empathic way rather than a judgmental, dismissive or confrontational one.' },
    { id: 'c04', tag: 'Exhibit 4.1', q: 'What are the three areas of counselor focus in Precontemplation?', a: 'Develop rapport and trust; raise doubts and concerns about substance use; understand special considerations for clients mandated to treatment.' },
    { id: 'c05', tag: 'Rapport', q: 'Why ask permission before raising the topic of changing substance use?', a: 'It shows respect for the client’s autonomy.' },
    { id: 'c06', tag: 'Rapport', q: 'What three categories of information should you clarify when raising confidentiality?', a: 'What stays private, what can be released with permission, and what must be sent back to a referring agency.' },
    { id: 'c07', tag: 'Rapport', q: 'A defensive client won’t talk about his drinking but mentions a heavy workload. What can you do?', a: 'Use that topic of interest and link it to substance use: “How does your use of alcohol fit into this?”' },
    { id: 'c08', tag: 'Rapport', q: 'Why avoid calling it the client’s “problem” early on?', a: 'It may not reflect the client’s perspective, and labels raise defenses.' },
    { id: 'c09', tag: 'Intoxication', q: 'A client arrives intoxicated. What does TIP 35 advise?', a: 'Nonjudgmentally ask the client to leave, reschedule, and help the client get home safely — per an agency policy you explained in the first session.' },
    { id: 'c10', tag: 'Perception', q: 'Give an open question to elicit the client’s perception of why he is here.', a: '“Can you tell me a bit about what brings you here today?” or “I’d like to understand your perspective on why you’re here. Can we start there?”' },
    { id: 'c11', tag: 'Emotions', q: 'Why must you acknowledge the emotions a client arrives with?', a: 'Strong emotions (shaken, angry, ashamed, terrified, relieved) become obstacles to change if not acknowledged through reflective listening.' },
    { id: 'c12', tag: 'External pressure', q: 'A client says his probation officer is the problem. What question raises awareness without arguing?', a: '“Why do you think your probation officer believes you have a problem?” — then reflect, neither agreeing nor disagreeing.' },
    { id: 'c13', tag: 'External pressure', q: 'A wife insisted on treatment; the client denies a problem. What can you ask?', a: '“What kind of things seem to bother her?” or “What do you think makes her believe there is a problem associated with your drinking?” If views differ, invite her to a session.' },
    { id: 'c14', tag: 'Assessment', q: 'Why shouldn’t you assume a client with obvious SUD signs is ready to change?', a: 'Clients can be anywhere on the severity continuum and at any stage of readiness; strategy must follow assessed readiness.' },
    { id: 'c15', tag: 'Rulers', q: 'Where do clients in Precontemplation usually fall on the Importance and Confidence Rulers?', a: 'At the low end, generally 0 to 3.' },
    { id: 'c16', tag: 'Rulers', q: 'What does each ruler indicate?', a: 'Importance Ruler: how important change is right now. Confidence Ruler: self-efficacy for change right now. Together: readiness.' },
    { id: 'c17', tag: '5 Rs', q: 'Name the 5 Rs of Precontemplation.', a: 'Reveling, Reluctance, Rebellion, Resignation, Rationalization (DiClemente, 2018).' },
    { id: 'c18', tag: '5 Rs', q: 'A young client focuses on how fun his weekends of drinking are and has had no consequences. Which style, and approach?', a: 'Reveling — objective, nonjudgmental feedback about use and risks to raise doubt.' },
    { id: 'c19', tag: '5 Rs', q: 'A client fears losing control and is heavily invested in her drug. Which style, and approach?', a: 'Rebellion — emphasize personal choice and responsibility so she chooses rather than rebels.' },
    { id: 'c20', tag: '5 Rs', q: 'A client with five prior treatments says, “Nothing works for me.” Which style, and approach?', a: 'Resignation — restore hope: explore barriers and past successes with other behaviors, share that treatment helps people who thought they couldn’t change, link to people in recovery.' },
    { id: 'c21', tag: '5 Rs', q: 'Which style responds best to double-sided reflection rather than arguing?', a: 'Rationalization — clients who think they have all the answers.' },
    { id: 'c22', tag: '5 Rs', q: 'What two things help clients in the Reluctance style?', a: 'Nonjudgmental feedback about how use affects their lives, and reassurance that they can function without the addictive behavior.' },
    { id: 'c23', tag: 'Instruments', q: 'URICA: how many items, and which stages?', a: '32 items, 8 per stage — Precontemplation, Contemplation, Action, Maintenance; rated 1–5. The most common clinical measure of stage.' },
    { id: 'c24', tag: 'Instruments', q: 'How do you track a client’s movement through the SOC with the URICA?', a: 'Have the client fill it out periodically, each time keeping a named specific “problem” in mind.' },
    { id: 'c25', tag: 'Instruments', q: 'SOCRATES 8A vs 8D?', a: '8A is for alcohol, 8D for drugs (19-item clinical versions).' },
    { id: 'c26', tag: 'Instruments', q: 'What three areas do the SOCRATES short-version items assess?', a: 'Problem recognition, ambivalence, and taking steps.' },
    { id: 'c27', tag: 'Instruments', q: 'Who is the Readiness To Change Questionnaire for, and how is it built?', a: 'Healthcare providers who aren’t addiction specialists, for alcohol; 12 items adapted from the URICA across 3 stages (Precontemplation, Contemplation, Action).' },
    { id: 'c28', tag: 'Commend', q: 'Affirm a mother who came to treatment rather than risk losing custody.', a: '“You must care very much about your child.”' },
    { id: 'c29', tag: 'Direction', q: 'Must you agree on treatment goals at the start with a client in Precontemplation?', a: 'No. Agree on how to proceed in the current conversation (e.g., agenda mapping).' },
    { id: 'c30', tag: 'Information', q: 'List the tips for providing information about effects and risks.', a: 'Use EPE; ask permission or what they know; talk about any user, not just the client; state what experts found; small chunks then elicit; describe addiction biologically.' },
    { id: 'c31', tag: 'Information', q: 'Why describe addiction in biological terms?', a: 'Understanding the facts of addiction can increase hope as well as readiness to change.' },
    { id: 'c32', tag: 'Expert comment', q: 'In the liver-transplant example, why not confront the client with the overwhelming evidence?', a: 'It makes him more defensive, reinforces denial, and strengthens guilt and shame.' },
    { id: 'c33', tag: 'Expert comment', q: 'In the liver-transplant example, what represents the move from Precontemplation to Contemplation?', a: 'Self-diagnosis, prompted by tailored psychoeducation on the biology of alcohol dependence.' },
    { id: 'c34', tag: 'Written materials', q: 'Which words should be avoided in brochures and other written materials?', a: 'Judgmental language such as “abuse” or “denial”; also avoid opening with a long list of rules.' },
    { id: 'c35', tag: 'Concern', q: 'In exploring the “positive” and “less-positive” aspects of use, where do you start?', a: 'With the client’s view of the benefits, then move to less-beneficial aspects.' },
    { id: 'c36', tag: 'Concern', q: 'What does it mean if a client can name nothing he “likes less” about drinking?', a: 'He is probably not ready to consider change and may need more information.' },
    { id: 'c37', tag: 'Concern', q: 'Why not spend too long on the “good” things about use?', a: 'It can reinforce sustain talk, which is linked to lower motivation and worse outcomes.' },
    { id: 'c38', tag: 'Feedback', q: 'Against whom should personalized feedback compare the client’s consumption?', a: 'Others of the same culture, age or gender.' },
    { id: 'c39', tag: 'Feedback', q: 'When is the best time for a formal assessment?', a: 'After at least one session with you, or with a brief MI conversation before and after it.' },
    { id: 'c40', tag: 'Feedback', q: 'How much feedback may be needed to move a client toward Contemplation?', a: 'Sometimes only one or two pieces, delivered in small chunks with EPE.' },
    { id: 'c41', tag: 'Typical day', q: 'Key rules for the “typical day” technique?', a: 'Ask permission; be curious; avoid “problem” unless the client uses it; follow behaviors and feelings through the day (“What happens when…?”); ask slowly; let the client use his own words.' },
    { id: 'c42', tag: 'SOs', q: 'What four steps come before involving an SO?', a: 'Ask the client’s permission, describe the benefits, review confidentiality, and obtain written releases.' },
    { id: 'c43', tag: 'SOs', q: 'What should you check about an SO before involving him or her?', a: 'A positive relationship with the client, genuine investment in change, and whether the SO supports the client’s motivational statements.' },
    { id: 'c44', tag: 'SOs', q: 'The SO keeps criticizing the client in session. What do you do?', a: 'Refocus the conversation; if the SO can’t be supportive and nonconfrontational, limit the role to information sharing and refer the SO for support.' },
    { id: 'c45', tag: 'SOs', q: 'How can you involve an SO who cannot attend?', a: 'Invite the SO figuratively — evoke change talk about the importance of that person: “How would not drinking impact the quality of your relationship with your daughter?”' },
    { id: 'c46', tag: 'Door open', q: 'The client stays in Precontemplation. How do you close the session?', a: 'Summarize your concern factually, respect the decision and personal choice, give contact and crisis information, and ask permission for a check-in call in a month.' },
    { id: 'c47', tag: 'Mandated', q: 'What common mistake do counselors make with mandated clients?', a: 'Using Action stage interventions immediately, though most are in Precontemplation — counterproductive.' },
    { id: 'c48', tag: 'Mandated', q: 'What should you do with mandated clients’ sustain talk before evoking change talk?', a: 'Lessen or “soften” it.' },
    { id: 'c49', tag: 'Mandated', q: 'How do mandated clients’ outcomes compare with voluntary clients’?', a: 'Similar, when stage-appropriate motivational strategies are used.' },
    { id: 'c50', tag: 'Mandated', q: 'What does “decontaminating” the referral mean?', a: 'Spending the first session addressing feelings about the referral — honoring anger and powerlessness, avoiding assumptions, and offering to explore what the client finds useful (“I’m sorry you came through the door this way”).' },
    { id: 'c51', tag: 'Mandated', q: 'How should you present consequences of non-participation?', a: 'Neutrally; acknowledge ambivalence; separate your role from the agency’s; don’t take sides on fairness; emphasize personal choice.' }
  ],
  quiz: [
    { id: 'q01', q: 'Which is the counselor’s FIRST focus with a client in Precontemplation?', choices: ['Developing rapport and trust', 'Setting abstinence goals', 'Teaching relapse prevention skills', 'Confronting denial'], answer: 0, why: 'Before raising change with someone not thinking about it, establish rapport and trust.' },
    { id: 'q02', q: 'A client says his probation officer “has it in for him.” The best response is:', choices: ['“She’s just doing her job.”', '“That sounds unfair to me.”', '“Why do you think your probation officer believes you have a problem?”', '“Let’s not talk about her.”'], answer: 2, why: 'It lets the client express the referral source’s view without the counselor agreeing or disagreeing.' },
    { id: 'q03', q: 'Clients in Precontemplation typically score where on the Importance and Confidence Rulers?', choices: ['7–10', '4–6', 'Exactly 5', '0–3'], answer: 3, why: 'They are generally at the low end, 0 to 3.' },
    { id: 'q04', q: 'A client feels hopeless after many failed attempts to quit. Which of the 5 Rs is this?', choices: ['Reveling', 'Resignation', 'Rationalization', 'Reluctance'], answer: 1, why: 'Resignation — hopeless, helpless, overwhelmed; the task is to restore hope.' },
    { id: 'q05', q: 'For a client in the Rebellion style, the most useful emphasis is:', choices: ['Personal choice and responsibility', 'Detailed risk statistics', 'Reassurance about functioning without the substance', 'Amplified reflections'], answer: 0, why: 'Rebellious clients fear losing control; emphasizing choice helps them choose rather than rebel.' },
    { id: 'q06', q: 'Which strategy does TIP 35 call most effective for Rationalization?', choices: ['Providing a handbook', 'Arguing with logic', 'Double-sided reflection', 'Referral to a higher level of care'], answer: 2, why: 'Acknowledge what they say and point out earlier reservations, rather than arguing.' },
    { id: 'q07', q: 'Which instrument has 32 items with 8 per stage and is the most common clinical measure of SOC stage?', choices: ['SOCRATES 8D', 'AUDIT', 'Readiness To Change Questionnaire', 'URICA'], answer: 3, why: 'URICA: Precontemplation, Contemplation, Action, Maintenance subscales.' },
    { id: 'q08', q: 'The Readiness To Change Questionnaire is designed for:', choices: ['Healthcare providers who are not addiction specialists', 'Court evaluators', 'Family members', 'Adolescents only'], answer: 0, why: '12 items adapted from the URICA, for alcohol, across 3 stages.' },
    { id: 'q09', q: 'When providing information about substance effects, you should:', choices: ['Say what you personally believe happens', 'Focus on what will happen to this client specifically', 'Give all the information at once', 'State what experts have found and talk about any user'], answer: 3, why: 'Depersonalized, expert-based information in small chunks is less threatening.' },
    { id: 'q10', q: 'In the liver-transplant expert comment, the client’s move to Contemplation came through:', choices: ['Confrontation with lab results', 'Self-diagnosis after tailored psychoeducation', 'A family intervention', 'Being refused a transplant listing'], answer: 1, why: 'Seeing himself in the biological picture of addiction led to self-diagnosis.' },
    { id: 'q11', q: 'When exploring the “positive” and “less-positive” aspects of use, you should:', choices: ['Start with problems to create urgency', 'Avoid asking what the client likes about use', 'Start with perceived benefits, then ask what the client likes less', 'Spend most of the time on benefits'], answer: 2, why: 'Starting with benefits shows understanding; too long on benefits reinforces sustain talk.' },
    { id: 'q12', q: '“Help me understand. You see no need to change, and you are concerned about losing your family. I don’t see how this fits together.” This is:', choices: ['Balanced reflective listening', 'A closed question', 'Agreement with a twist', 'Giving advice'], answer: 0, why: 'It holds both sides and invites the client to resolve the inconsistency.' },
    { id: 'q13', q: 'Ideally, a formal assessment is scheduled:', choices: ['Before any conversation, to save time', 'After at least one session, or wrapped in brief MI conversations', 'Only if the client requests it', 'Only for mandated clients'], answer: 1, why: 'This builds rapport and gauges readiness and response to feedback.' },
    { id: 'q14', q: 'In the “typical day” technique, the counselor should:', choices: ['Use the word “problem” early', 'Suggest reasons why events happened', 'Skip the client’s feelings', 'Ask permission, be curious, and follow behaviors and feelings through the day'], answer: 3, why: 'Avoid “problem” unless the client uses it; don’t add your own explanations.' },
    { id: 'q15', q: 'Which must happen BEFORE contacting a client’s SO?', choices: ['Client permission and written releases', 'A family therapy referral', 'A urine test', 'An Al-Anon meeting'], answer: 0, why: 'Ask permission, describe benefits, review confidentiality, obtain releases.' },
    { id: 'q16', q: 'An SO in session repeatedly criticizes and shames the client. The best approach is to:', choices: ['Let the SO vent fully', 'Refocus, and if it persists, limit the SO’s role to information sharing', 'Side with the SO', 'End the client’s treatment'], answer: 1, why: 'Negative SO feedback that reinforces sustain talk should be refocused or limited.' },
    { id: 'q17', q: 'A client remains in Precontemplation after assessment. TIP 35 recommends that you:', choices: ['Discharge without follow-up', 'Insist on inpatient admission', 'Express concern, respect the choice, and ask permission for a check-in call in a month', 'Repeat the full assessment immediately'], answer: 2, why: 'Express concern and leave the door open.' },
    { id: 'q18', q: 'With mandated clients, which is counterproductive?', choices: ['Honoring anger', 'Differentiating your role from the agency', 'Emphasizing personal choice', 'Starting Action stage interventions immediately'], answer: 3, why: 'Most mandated clients are in Precontemplation.' },
    { id: 'q19', q: 'Compared with voluntary clients, mandated clients’ treatment outcomes are:', choices: ['Much worse', 'Similar', 'Better only with confrontation', 'Unknown'], answer: 1, why: 'Similar outcomes when stage-appropriate strategies are used.' },
    { id: 'q20', q: 'In Exhibit 4.3, the counselor tells Paul, “It seems to me you’ve made some good choices so far.” This is mainly:', choices: ['A warning', 'A closed question', 'A reframe that affirms his choices', 'Agreeing with his view of the probation officer'], answer: 2, why: 'It reframes coming to the appointment as a good choice, affirming autonomy.' }
  ],
  glossary: [
    { term: '5 Rs', def: 'DiClemente’s five styles of sustain talk in Precontemplation: Reveling, Reluctance, Rebellion, Resignation, Rationalization.', alt: ['five Rs'] },
    { term: 'Reveling', def: '5 Rs style: still focused on the good experiences of use, few consequences yet. Approach: objective, nonjudgmental feedback.' },
    { term: 'Reluctance', def: '5 Rs style: lacking knowledge of the problem’s dimensions or personal impact. Approach: feedback plus reassurance they can function without the substance.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Rebellion', def: '5 Rs style: fear of losing control and heavy investment in the substance. Approach: emphasize personal choice and responsibility.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Resignation', def: '5 Rs style: hopeless and overwhelmed after many attempts. Approach: restore hope; explore barriers and past successes; link to people in recovery.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'Rationalization', def: '5 Rs style: “use is a problem for others, not me.” Approach: double-sided reflection rather than arguing.', nolink: true },
    { term: 'URICA', def: 'University of Rhode Island Change Assessment Scale: 32 items, 8 per stage (Precontemplation, Contemplation, Action, Maintenance); the most common clinical measure of SOC stage.', alt: ['University of Rhode Island Change Assessment Scale'] },
    { term: 'SOCRATES', def: 'Stages of Change Readiness and Treatment Eagerness Scale; 19-item clinical version (8A alcohol, 8D drugs) assessing problem recognition, ambivalence and taking steps.' },
    { term: 'Readiness To Change Questionnaire', def: '12-item alcohol readiness tool adapted from the URICA for non-specialist healthcare providers; maps to Precontemplation, Contemplation and Action.' },
    { term: 'Significant other', def: 'A person with a close personal relationship to the client — typically a parent, spouse, live-in partner or other family member.', alt: ['significant others', 'SOs'] },
    { term: 'Mandated treatment', def: 'Treatment the client is ordered to attend by an employer, employee assistance program or the criminal justice system, with consequences for not participating.', alt: ['mandated to treatment', 'mandated clients'] },
    { term: 'Decontaminating the referral', def: 'Using the first session with a mandated client to address feelings about how he or she was referred — honoring anger and powerlessness.', alt: ['decontaminating'] },
    { term: 'Typical day', def: 'An informal assessment in which the client walks through a typical day, behaviors and feelings, so the counselor learns the context of use.', alt: ['description of a typical day'], nolink: true },
    { term: 'Self-diagnosis', def: 'The client’s own recognition of having an SUD, often after tailored psychoeducation; itself a move from Precontemplation to Contemplation.' },
    { term: 'Balanced reflective listening', def: 'Reflecting both sides of what the client has said and expressing honest puzzlement about how they fit together.' },
    { term: 'Release of information', def: 'Written consent, meeting federal confidentiality rules, specifying what information may be shared with a referring agency or SO.' }
  ]
});
